<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Berwick-upon-Tweed</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Berwick-upon-Tweed is a town at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tweed">River Tweed</a>, now the northernmost town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In the thirteenth century it was the richest Scottish burgh and the kingdom's main port for wool exports. Edward I sacked it in 1296 at the start of the Wars of Scottish Independence, and after changing hands many times it has been English since 1482.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed.jpg" alt="Berwick-upon-Tweed" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Walter Baxter · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berwick-upon-Tweed_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6643184.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal burghs such as <b>Berwick</b>, Perth, Aberdeen and Edinburgh held monopolies of foreign trade, exporting wool, hides, fish and later coal and salt to the Low Countries, the Baltic and France; from the fifteenth century a Scottish staple port in the Netherlands, from 1541 at Veere, handled much…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Scottish-held town of <b>Berwick-upon-Tweed</b> surrendered to King Edward III of England after a four-month siege, once a Scottish relief army had been heavily defeated at Halidon Hill.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Edward I of England sacked <b>Berwick-upon-Tweed</b>, then the richest Scottish burgh and the kingdom's main port for wool exports.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
